astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_013,13,"ore are small. Our ideais, therefore, to retain the exact dependence ofV one and α at each order, avoiding the expansion ine carriedonin the classical Laplace approach. The resulting expansionis expectedto converge better than the classical developmentsin regimes where bothe and α are small, orin intermediate regimes where neither parameteris particularly small but their product remains small. These regimes define the natural domainof interestof the present formulation, whichis explored quantitativelyin Sect. 4.Moreover, exact dependence one and α at each order should allowusto span the two regimese →0 and α →0 ina single formulation. In the following, we construct the Laplace–Legendre expansionat zeroth, first, and arbitrary order, focusingin each caseon the expressionof the secular direct partof the disturbing function.",3 Laplace-Legendre expansion,3 Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,154,118,False,False,140,258,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_014,14,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion The convergenceof truncated secular expansionsof the disturbing function has been investigatedin several previous studies, primarily through numerical assessmentsof the accuracyof high-order expansions. In (Laskar 1985), the convergenceof the Laplace expansionat second orderof the massesis analyzed for all planetary pairsin the Solar System with respectto the truncationin the harmonicsof the first order expansion. For the coplanar 3-body problem, Libert & Henrard (2005) considered Hamiltonian expansions carriedto degree 12 in eccentricity and showed that the resulting secular models accurately reproduce the long-term dynamicsof exoplanetary systems with large eccentricities, while also demonstrating that the seriesof secular terms con verges better than the series associated with the full perturbation. Migaszewski & Go´zdziewski (2008) performeda similar convergence studyof the Legendre expansion, extendingitupto order 24 in the semi-major axis ratio and applyingit toa rangeof exoplanetary configurations. They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while the convergence becomes progressively sloweras the system becomes more compact. In this section, we compare the convergence propertiesof the Laplace–Legendre expansion introducedin Sect. 3 with thoseof",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,True,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_015,15,"exoplanetary configurations. They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while the convergence becomes progressively sloweras the system becomes more compact. In this section, we compare the convergence propertiesof the Laplace–Legendre expansion introducedin Sect. 3 with thoseof the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions. In line with the aforementioned studies, we focus here ona numerical assessmentof convergence basedon the evaluationof the seriesat diﬀerent truncation orders. In the celebrated chapter VIII on Formal calculus, in LesM´ethodes Nouvellesde laM´ecaniqueC´eleste, Poincar´e (1892) distinguishes the convergencein the sensof the astronomers and mathematicians, and reminds that both approaches are legitimate. As discussedin Sect. 2.2, we know that for suﬃciently small valuesof the ratioof semi major axis, α, eccentricities and inclinations, the series expansion (2.2) is convergent. Butin the present work, we consider formal expansions and address the convergence questions ina numerical way, in the senseof the astronomers, without addressing more mathematical questions suchas the domainof convergenceof the series, the sizeof the remainder, or the decay rateof the terms. Our goalisto assess the accuracyof each seriesin approximating the secular direct partof the perturbing function,",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_016,16,"isa trade-oﬀfor the optimalityof the new expansionin the regimee, α →0. Anyway, the clear advantageof the new expansionis that nowwe havea global formulation that bridges the Laplace and Leg endre regimes. This broader applicability comesat the cost ofa larger numberof terms retained ata given expansion order comparedto the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions (see AppendixB). Finally, we note that when bothe and α are not small (e.g, e, α > 0.2), the convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion deteriorates and becomes progressively slower, similarlyto the classical expansions. This behavioris expected, asin such",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,117,90,False,False,1540,1630,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_017,17,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian Aya Alnajjarine1*, Jacques Laskar1* and Federico Mogavero2,1 1LTE, CNRS, Observatoirede Paris, Universit´e PSL, Sorbonne Universit´e, 77 Avenue Denfert-Rochereau, 75014 Paris, France. 2Dipartimentodi Matematica “Tullio Levi-Civita”, Universit`a degli Studidi Padova, Via Trieste 63, 35121 Padova, Italy. *Corresponding author(s). E-mail(s): aya.alnajjarine@obspm.fr; jacques.laskar@obspm.fr; Contributing authors: mogavero@math.unipd.it;",Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian,Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,73,56,False,False,0,56,False,1.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_000,0,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs their positions and velocities (e.g. eclipticvs equato rial). Our focusis ratheron the choiceof coordinates usedto split offa dominant Keplerian term from the full Hamiltonianas partof the WH algorithm. The two most commonly used coordinate systems for the Hamil tonian splitting are Jacobi coordinates and democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC). Both coordinate sys tems have advantages and disadvantages. For example, DHC (M. J. Duncanetal. 1998) canbe used forhy brid symplectic integrators and canbe more easily par allelized (P. Javaherietal. 2023). Jacobi coordinateson the other hand are more accurate and canbe used with symplectic correctorsto achieve very high order (J. Wisdometal. 1996; S. Blanesetal. 2013). Fora pre cise definition anda more detailed comparisonof these coordinate systems seeH. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). Both coordinate systems have been usedto study the long-term evolution and stabilityof the Solar System. The first direct integrations over 5 Gyr were performed byJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) with the SABA integrator, a high order",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_001,1,"D. Tamayo (2019). Both coordinate systems have been usedto study the long-term evolution and stabilityof the Solar System. The first direct integrations over 5 Gyr were performed byJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) with the SABA integrator, a high order integrator using Jacobi coordi nates (J. Laskar & P. Robutel 2001). More recently, N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) used the MER CURY hybrid integrator (J. E. Chambers 1999) with Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea long history (seee.g. W. J. Eckertetal. 1951 for oneof the first simulationsof this kind, andJ. Laskar 2013 fora historical summaryof the field). Although dif ferent authors use different numerical algorithms, most modern long-term simulations use symplectic integra tors that make useof the Wisdom-Holman splitting (J. L. Wisdom 1981; J. Wisdom & M. Holman 1991; H. Kinoshitaetal. 1991). Various variationsof the Wisdom-Holman (WH) algorithm exist, differingin the orderof the scheme, the coordinate system used for the Hamiltonian splitting (D. M. Hernandez & W. Dehnen 2017), and other details suchas whetheror not symplec tic correctors are used (fora comparisonsof methods seeH. Reinetal. 2019). Several",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_002,2,"algorithm exist, differingin the orderof the scheme, the coordinate system used for the Hamiltonian splitting (D. M. Hernandez & W. Dehnen 2017), and other details suchas whetheror not symplec tic correctors are used (fora comparisonsof methods seeH. Reinetal. 2019). Several authors have argued thatitis important for the adopted timesteptobe short enoughto resolve peri center passages, which becomes challengingat highor bital eccentricities like those achieved when Mercury goes unstable (K. P. Rauch & M. Holman 1999; J. Wis dom 2015; D. M. Hernandezetal. 2022). This paper shows that this criterion dependson the coordinate system used. To clarify, wedo not referto the DHC to study the influenceof passing field starson the Solar System. Notably, the simulations that constitute the control group ofN. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) and shouldbe comparableto those ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) showan instability rateof only 0.1%, whereas the simulations ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) and others (R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023) resultinan instability ratean orderof magnitude larger, ∼1%. Similar discrepancies were also observed byR. E. Zeebe",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_003,3,"only 0.1%, whereas the simulations ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) and others (R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023) resultinan instability ratean orderof magnitude larger, ∼1%. Similar discrepancies were also observed byR. E. Zeebe (2015) who studied the position errorof Mer cury when using different Hamiltonian splittings. R. E. Zeebe (2015) correctly concluded that DHC coordinates lack accuracy and Jacobi coordinates are better suitedto the problem. We beginin Sec. 2.1 by running numerical experi ments, which show that this discrepancyis indeed dueto the different coordinate systems usedin the Hamiltonian splitting. We show that simulations using DHC requirea significantly smaller timestepin ordertobe statisti cally converged. In Sec. 2.2, we demonstrate that the stoodin termsof errors notin the position but ratherin the precession rate, as the Solar System’s evolutionis primarily governedby secular dynamics. With this understanding, we show that integrations using DHC become unreliable whena planet’s eccentricity becomes large, or more precisely when the pericenter crossing timescale becomes comparableto the adopted timestep. We showin Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effectof the arti ficial numerical precessionis the",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_004,4,"we show that integrations using DHC become unreliable whena planet’s eccentricity becomes large, or more precisely when the pericenter crossing timescale becomes comparableto the adopted timestep. We showin Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effectof the arti ficial numerical precessionis the sameas the stabilizing effect dueto general relativistic (GR) precession. By contrast, the splitting using Jacobi coordinates solves the two-body problem for the inner-most planet exactly and reliably reproduces the correct precession rate outto much larger eccentricities. This allows fora signif icantly longer timestep when using Jacobi coordinatesas comparedto DHC. In Sec. 3 we discuss how the pre cession error originates from the integrator’s jump step. We concludein Sec. 4 with some general advice. integrator with DHC. This helps rule out any errorsin the implementation asa cause for the observed discrep ancies. For all simulationswe use the present-day positions and velocitiesof all eight Solar System planetsas initial conditions. We combine each planet’s mass with thatof any satellites intoa single point particle locatedat the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. Wedo",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,560,740,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_005,5,"all eight Solar System planetsas initial conditions. We combine each planet’s mass with thatof any satellites intoa single point particle locatedat the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. Wedo however include general relativis tic correctionsin the formofan additional potential (A. Nobili & I. W. Roxburgh 1986; D. Tamayoetal. 2020) as the additional precession rateis very importantto correctly capture the long-term stabilityof the Solar System (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). Numerical experiments byD. S. Abbotetal. (2023) suggest that this simplified physical setup incorporates all the relevant physicsto capture thein stability rateof Mercury. We determine ifa systemis unstableby monitoring the eccentricityof planets. Specifically, we definea sys temas unstableifan eccentricity (most likely thatof Mercury) reaches 0.7 at any time during the integra tion. We ran additional tests with different instability criteria, but theydo not change the results significantly (see also Table 1 of the Supplementary Information ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009).",1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,1.INTRODUCTION coordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,218,167,False,False,700,867,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_006,6,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja cobi coordinates perform better than DHC in Solar Sys tem integrations. Thisis because when using Jacobico ordinates for the Hamiltonian splitting, Mercury’smo tionis decomposed intoa Keplerian step ona two-body orbit around the Sun, andan interaction step with the other planets. In the limit where Mercury hasa high eccentricity, the Kepler solver still correctly solves for the Keplerian motion around the Sun-Mercury barycen ter for arbitrarily large timesteps. And because both the orbital timescales and the timescales for planet planet interactions are much longer than the pericenter resolving Tf (see Fig. 1). DHC on the other hand split the Hamiltonian into three parts: Keplerian motion, planet-planet interac tions, andaso-called jump stepor solar term (seeM. J. Duncanetal. 1998; H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2019). The jump step dependson Mercury’s momentum (and thatof the other planets) relativeto the Solar System barycenter. The Keplerian motion solved forin the Ke pler stepis also different from Jacobi case. Mercury does not",3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_007,7,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have been reportedin the literature, ranging from ∼0.1% to 1% (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023; N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond 2025). In this paperwe traced this discrepancy backto the coordinate system usedin the Hamiltonian splittingof the Wisdom-Holman inte grator. We showed that simulations using democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC) requirea significantly smaller timestepin ordertobe statistically converged comparedto simulations using Jacobi coordinates. The reason for this slow convergenceis the artificial numeri cal precession introducedby DHC whichis stronglyec centricity and timestep dependent. Our results show that DHC should notbe usedin sim ulations which require high accuracy during times when",4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,173,133,False,False,0,133,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_008,8,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof the Solar System with different integrators, Hamilto nian splittings, and timesteps and measure the insta bility ratein each set. Each simulationis slightly per turbedby changing the positionof Earthon the order ofa few metersto obtaina setof equally valid realizations. The results are shownin Table 2, together with previous resultsin the literature. We see that integrations using Jacobi coordinates all agree with the instability rateof ∼1% determined byJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009). However, our simula tions with DHC and timestepsof ∼6 days suppress all instabilities. The fact thatwe reproduce this result with different integrators and agree with the integrations ofN. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) is reassuringasit shows this effectis not due toa specific implementation. Importantly, we are ableto reproduce the correctin stability rate with DHC simulations that usean orderof magnitude smaller timestepof 0.6 days (bottom row). timestep. We will show more evidence for this inter pretationin the next section.","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_009,9,"coordinate systems and timesteps. This timewe also vary the general rela sion), to 1 (normal GR precession), to 2 (double the GR precession). We run 100 simulations for each case and slightly perturb again the positionof Earthto obtaina setof equally valid realizations. This setup letsus quickly measure the instability rate foran ensembleof simulations. We choseto restart sim ulations 5 Myrs before the instability, roughly one Lya punov timescale (J. Laskar 1989), to give individual sim ulations timeto diverge slightly through phase space. If (1 −e1)2 Tf ≡2π 1 −e2 , (1) n1 p where n1 is the mean motionof Mercury (J. Wisdom 2015). Note that the pericenter timescale canbe sig nificantly shorter than the orbital period for eccentric orbits. One can see that the WH integrator with Jacobico ordinates does not result ina significant precessioner ror for timesteps ≲10 days. On the other hand, the WH integrator with DHC introducesan artificialnu merical precession whichis strongly dependenton both the timestep and the eccentricityof Mercury. For large eccentricities, the artificial numerical precession ratebe comes larger than the GR precession rate. Previous e1",2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_010,10,"the simulations with Ja cobi coordinates, we still seean instability rateof 100%. This shows that the signof this change matters. More over, because the artificial numerical precession presentin DHC simulationsisin the same directionas the GR precession, we can conclude that the artificial precessionis making the simulations more stablein the same way that the GR precession does. Coordinates Timestep GR Instability rate Jacobi 6 days 1.0 100% Jacobi 6 days 2.0 0% Jacobi 6 days 0.0 100% DHC 6 days 1.0 0% DHC 0.6 days 1.0 100% we wereto restart them much earlier, then the small per turbationswe addto the initial conditions would move most trajectories away from the high eccentricity phasewe aimto explore. Our results are shownin Table 2. First, note that justasin Sect. 2.1, when usinga 6 day timestep, DHC inte case, but rather the Solar System barycenter5. In the limitof high eccentricity, the difference between Mer cury’s true trajectory and the Keplerian orbit the Ke pler solver solves for becomes significant especially near pericenter. The error ofa symplectic integrator (or more gener allyan operator splitting method) canbe understoodin",2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_011,11,"the limitof high eccentricity, the difference between Mer cury’s true trajectory and the Keplerian orbit the Ke pler solver solves for becomes significant especially near pericenter. The error ofa symplectic integrator (or more gener allyan operator splitting method) canbe understoodin termsofan infinite sumof commutators consistingof compositionsof the integrator’s individual steps (seee.g. P. Saha & S. Tremaine 1992; H. Reinetal. 2019; D. Tamayoetal. 2020). The commutators involving the jump step leadto error terms not presentin the caseof Jacobi coordinates anditis those commutators which leadto the artificial numerical precession. The comb like structure seenin the curve for DHC in Fig. 1 is dueto strong timestep resonances (K. P. Rauch & M. Hol man 1999). To suppress these timestep resonances and keep the artificial numerical precession rate below thatof GR we see thatwe needto resolve Tf withat leasta few (∼4) timesteps. The effectwe discuss primarily affects the innermost planet, i.e. Mercuryin the caseof the Solar System. Venus, for example, hasa 2.6 times longer orbital period than Mercury. We are guaranteedto resolve Venus’ peri center timescaleupto eccentricitiesof e2 ∼0.4 as longaswe resolve Mercury’s orbital",2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_012,12,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 5 More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen tric velocities. SeeM. J. Duncanetal. (1998); H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). the eccentricityof planetsis high. The Solar Systemis one example where high accuracyis requiredto measure the correct instability rate because the eccentricityof Mercury becomes large (≳0.4) just before instabilities occur and numerical errors can suppress the behaviour. Reducing the timestepin simulations using DHC is one possible solution. Another, pursued byH. F. Lev ison & M. J. Duncan (2000) andT. Luetal. (2024) isto shift the problematic termsin the jump step during close pericenter passages into the Keplerian step andin tegrateit using Bulirsch-Stoer (J. E. Chambers 1999). However, we note thata simple scheme using Jacobico ordinates can reproduce the correct instability rate for surprisingly large timesteps (H. Reinetal. 2025). Asa result, statistically converged simulations with DHC are significantly more computationally expensive than simulations using Jacobi coordinatesso that Jacobico ordinates are advantageousin most scenarios. We have focused our experiments and discussionon the Solar System. However, the general advice","5 More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen","5 More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_013,13,"Reinetal. 2025). Asa result, statistically converged simulations with DHC are significantly more computationally expensive than simulations using Jacobi coordinatesso that Jacobico ordinates are advantageousin most scenarios. We have focused our experiments and discussionon the Solar System. However, the general advice for run ning simulationsof any planetary system where one wantsto integrate untilan instability occursis the same: Jacobi coordinates perform significantly better. As soonas orbit crossing occurs and planets can have closeen tenat this pointa simulation canbe stopped and the system canbe considered unstable. Butifitis necessaryto integrate further, thena switch toa hybrid symplec tic integrator ora brute force non-symplectic integra tor canbe madeat this point. Since hybrid integrators workin DHC, itis importantto check that the timestepis small enough, at the very leastdt ≲Tf/4, or ideallydt ≲Tf/17 as suggested byJ. Wisdom (2015).","5 More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen","5 More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,169,130,False,False,140,270,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_014,14,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System Hanno Rein ,1, 2, 3 Kavi Dey ,4 and Daniel Tamayo 1Dept. of Physical and Environmental Sciences, Universityof Torontoat Scarborough, Toronto, Ontario, M1C 1A4, Canada 2Dept. of Physics, Universityof Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 3Dept. of Astronomy and Astrophysics, Universityof Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 4Dept. of Physics, Harvey Mudd College, 301 Platt Blvd., Claremont 91711, USA",Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,112,86,False,False,0,86,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_015,15,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND package (H. Rein & S. F. Liu 2012). REBOUND in cludes several integrators, in particular the WH integra tor WHFast (H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2015) which allowsusto choose the coordinate system for the Hamiltonian splitting. We also run simulations with the WHFast512 integrator (P. Javaherietal. 2023), which only supports DHC. We include comparisons with WHFast512 becauseit does not reuse any code from WHFast, and can there forebe seenasan independent implementation ofa WH Table 1. Comparisonof instability rates for 5 Gyr simulationsof the Solar System using Jacobi (top) vs. democratic heliocentric (bottom) coordinates, usinga rangeof integrators. Integrations are sortedin descending orderby timestep. Additional columns list the numberof runsin each simulation suite, as wellas whether the timestep was decreased once Mercury’s eccentricity rose abovea particular threshold (that variesby paper). The final column lists the percentageof casesin which Mercury goes unstable, with errors denoting 1σ counting statistics. Integrationsin Jacobi coordinates agree statisticallyon the instability rate overa broad rangeof timesteps,",2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_016,16,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen sitiveto GR and any additional precession (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). We therefore expecta significant effecton the stability rate from such numerical effects. We note that the artificial numerical precession has the same directionas the physical GR precession, and that the breakdownof the WH integrator with DHC occurs when the timestep becomes comparableto the pericenter-crossing timescale, dt ≳Tf/4. We thus con convergenceof simulations with DHC. We next runa setof simulations for 105 yrs with both DHC and Jacobi coordinates. We again use the present day values for all initial orbital parameters except for Mercury’s eccentricity e1 whichwe adjust manually this time. To measure the errorsof different schemes, we compare them against integrations using the high-order, adaptive-timestep IAS15 integrator (H. Rein & D. S. Spiegel 2015), which obtains correct resultsto within accumulated floating-point errors. In Fig. 1 we plot the absolute errorin the precession rateof Mercury asa functionof",2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,2.2.Artificial numerical precession studies have shown that the instability fractionis sen,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
