astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_000,0,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E Vasiliev 2025). The morphology and dynamical natureof the spiral arms are likewise uncertain (R. J. J. Grandetal. 2012; G. H. Hunteretal. 2024; Y. R. Khaliletal. 2025). Stellar tidal streams—formedby the gradual strippingof star clusters—provide phase-coherent tracersof the Galactic potential (S. E. Koposovetal. 2010; S. L. J. Gibbonsetal. 2014). Globular-cluster streams are typically narrow and prominent (e.g., M. Odenkirchenetal. 2001), but they often probe the potentialat larger Galactocentric distances. In contrast, open cluster streams residein the disk and are therefore par ticularly sensitiveto perturbations from the bar, spi ral arms, and encounters with giant molecular clouds (M. Gielesetal. 2006; K. Hattorietal. 2016; N. C. Amoriscoetal. 2016). Their low surface density and strong field-star contamination make them challengingto identify, but the astrometric precisionof Gaia now enables systematic searches for these nearby, diffuse structures. The Hyadesisan exceptional laboratorybe cause its proximity permitsa fine-grained comparison between observed tidal tails and dynamical models (S. Röseretal. 2011; Gaia Collaborationetal. 2018). Constraining the Milky Way gravitational poten tialis",1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_001,1,"Gaia now enables systematic searches for these nearby, diffuse structures. The Hyadesisan exceptional laboratorybe cause its proximity permitsa fine-grained comparison between observed tidal tails and dynamical models (S. Röseretal. 2011; Gaia Collaborationetal. 2018). Constraining the Milky Way gravitational poten tialis centralto connecting stellar kinematicsto the Galaxy’s mass distribution and secular evolution (J. Bland-Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016). Evenin the solar neighborhood, degeneracies persist between the halo shape and the disk and bulge/bar contributions. While the dark matter halois often approximated bya spherical NFW profile (J. F. Navarroetal. 1997), its precise geometry remainsa subjectof ongoingde bate. Many recent analyses favora flattened, oblate configuration (q < 1) to better match kinematic con straints (Y. Huangetal. 2024), although alternative models suggestinga prolate shape have also been pro posed (L. Zhuetal. 2026). In the disk, additionalun certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 kms−1 kpc−1) and “slow” (∼40 kms−1 kpc−1) so lutions supportedby different dynamical tracers (W. Dehnen 1999; J. L. Sandersetal. 2019; J. P. Clarke Corresponding author: Long Wang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cn Observational efforts initially focusedon the",1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_002,2,"pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 kms−1 kpc−1) and “slow” (∼40 kms−1 kpc−1) so lutions supportedby different dynamical tracers (W. Dehnen 1999; J. L. Sandersetal. 2019; J. P. Clarke Corresponding author: Long Wang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cn Observational efforts initially focusedon the Hyades core, using proper-motion catalogs and Hipparcosas trometryto characterize membership within the tidal radius (M. A. C. Perrymanetal. 1998). S. Röseretal. (2011) extended the search using the PPMXL catalog, providing early evidence that the cluster extends beyond its classical tidal boundary. The extended tails became accessible with Gaia ( Gaia Collaborationetal. 2016, 2018; S. Meingastetal. 2019). In particular, S. Röseretal. (2019) mapped tidal-tail membersin Gaia DR2 using the convergent point (CP) method, establishingan empirical reference for the stream orientation near the cluster. Numerical simulations are essential for connecting the present-day stream morphologyto the underlying Galac tic dynamics. EarlyN-body models primarily focusedon reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo lutionof its core properties withina static, axisymmet ric Galactic potential (Y. O. Chumaketal. 2005; A. Ernstetal. 2011). The Gaia detectionof asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com plex",1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_003,3,"focusedon reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo lutionof its core properties withina static, axisymmet ric Galactic potential (Y. O. Chumaketal. 2005; A. Ernstetal. 2011). The Gaia detectionof asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com plex than canbe capturedbyan axisymmetric model (T. Jerabkovaetal. 2021). Recent work has therefore begunto incorporate non-axisymmetric structure; for example, G. F. Thomasetal. (2023) explored the influence ofa rotating bar anda flattened halo. However, the joint impactof the bar and spiral arms—and, in particular, how their pattern speeds map into observable stream diagnostics—remains insuﬀiciently explored. In this paperwe performa systematic surveyof bar and spiral pattern speeds using high-resolution PETARN-body simulations ina multi-component AGAMA Milky Way potential. We confront the models with Gaia EDR3 using CP/CCP kinematic selection andan adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching procedure. We emphasizea single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution ina stream-aligned coordinate frame, includinga prominent density peakat Yrot ≈0.1 kpc. We find that models with Ωb ≃40–45 kms−1 kpc−1 and Ωs = 22.5 kms−1 kpc−1",1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,1.INTRODUCTION etal.2019a; S.Lucchinietal.2024; J.A.S.Hunt & E,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,231,177,False,True,420,597,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_004,4,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 5.CONCLUSIONS We modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream with directN-body simulations (PETAR) ina multi component Milky Way potential implemented with AGAMA and includinga rotating bar and spiral arms. By systematically varying the bar and spiral pattern speeds, we quantified how non-axisymmetric forcingre shapes the stream orientation, length, and internal den sity structure over the ∼648 Myr evolutionof the clus ter. We then confronted the models with observationsby combining CP/CCP-based kinematic selectionon Gaia EDR3 withan adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching technique. The key observational constraintis the stream-aligned longitudinal density profileof the Gaia candidates. This profile showsa prominent peakat Yrot ≈0.1 kpc, whichis consistent with the overdensity reported byT. Jer abkovaetal. (2021). We find that this featureis best reproducedby models with Ωs = 22.5 kms−1 kpc−1 and Ωb ≃40–45 kms−1 kpc−1, supportinga relatively slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that thein clusionof spiral armsis not negligible and Ωs around this intermediate value are favored. More broadly, our results demonstrate that nearby open-cluster streams providea complementary, local avenueto constrain",5.CONCLUSIONS We modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,5.CONCLUSIONS We modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_005,5,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per forming the simulations, data analysis, and writing the manuscript. LW campup with the initialre search concept, contributedto discussions, edited the manuscript, and supervised the overall project. TJ developed the observational-comparison concept, pro vided observational data, contributedto discussions, and edited the manuscript. ZH contributedto the Galactic-potential method development and discussions. Software: NumPy (C. R. Harrisetal. 2020), Matplotlib (J. D. Hunter 2007), Astropy ( As tropy Collaborationetal. 2013, 2018, 2022),","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,119,91,False,False,0,91,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_006,6,"kms−1 kpc−1, supportinga relatively slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that thein clusionof spiral armsis not negligible and Ωs around this intermediate value are favored. More broadly, our results demonstrate that nearby open-cluster streams providea complementary, local avenueto constrain non-axisymmetric Galactic dynamics using not only the global stream orientation but also its fine-grained den sity structure.",5.CONCLUSIONS We modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,5.CONCLUSIONS We modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,75,57,False,False,140,197,True,4.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_007,7,"Astropy SkyCoord packageto trans form the present-day parameters into Galactocentricpo sitions and velocities ( Astropy Collaborationetal. 2013, 2018, 2022). To simulate the dynamical evolutionof the Hyades stream, we first determined the initial phase-space coor dinatesof the cluster 648 Myr ago (L. Wang & T. Jer abkova 2021). For each gravitational potential model, we performeda backward integration ofa test particle starting from the cluster’s current Galactocentric posi tion. However, dueto internal dynamical interactions and centroid shifts inherent inN-body systems, thefi nal centroidof the simulated cluster typically deviates from the observed present-day position, even ifa test particle returnsto its origin. To account for this dis crepancy, we used the initial conditions derived from the test-particle integration asa first guess fora Nelder Mead optimization (J. A. Nelder & R. Mead 1965). We iteratively refined these initial coordinates until the sim ulated cluster’s centroidat the endof the 648 Myr evo lution satisfied the following convergence criteria: the positional differences inx andy were less than 100 pc, thez difference was less than 20 pc, the velocity mag nitude deviation was below 10 kms−1, and the",2.1.Galactic potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented,2.1.Galactic potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,False,3.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_008,8,"resulting config urations were remarkably similarto those obtainedin the bar-only simulations (Fig. 2), indicating that thein fluenceof the spiral armson the Hyadesis secondaryto thatof the bar. This resultis expected, asat the Galac tocentric distanceof the Hyades, the magnitudeof the gravitational potential contributedby the spiral armsis only approximately 1% of that contributedby the bar (G. H. Hunteretal. 2024). However, we note that for bar pattern speedsof 20 and 25 kms−1 kpc−1, the inclusionof spiral arms leads toa more discernible changein the stream orientation comparedto the bar-only case. This phenomenon likely arises froma resonant interaction between the bar and the spiral arms, as their respective pattern speedsin these specific models are closeto each other, enhancing the total non-axisymmetric perturbation experiencedby the cluster. Nevertheless, aswe showin Section 3, vary ing Ωs can still modulate the fine-grained density struc ture along the stream even when the global orientationis largely bar-driven. Furthermore, we compared these results with the base line model (see Table 1). In this axisymmetric case, the misalignment angle remains relatively small, further confirming that the significant deviations observedin our other simulation sets","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_009,9,"when the global orientationis largely bar-driven. Furthermore, we compared these results with the base line model (see Table 1). In this axisymmetric case, the misalignment angle remains relatively small, further confirming that the significant deviations observedin our other simulation sets are primarily drivenby the non-axisymmetric bar and spiral arms potential. We then fixed Ωbat 37.5 kms−1 kpc−1, a value con sistent with recent observational estimates, and varied Ωs (G. H. Hunteretal. 2024). The results are presentedin Fig. 3. Overall, the misalignment angle does notex hibit significant sensitivityto changesin Ωs; however, both the total lengthof the stellar stream and the emer","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,130,100,False,False,420,520,False,3.0
