astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_025,25,"0 disk, it isa poor analog for the detailed MW assembly history. Redshift Figure 2. Example accumulated fractionof the stellar and different DM components asa functionof redshift for m12i. The red curve represents all accreted stars. The green curve represents DM accreted from major mergers heavier than 109 M⊙and the blue curve shows the DM accreted either smoothly into the main haloor with unresolved small subhalos (< 109M⊙) lar particles and assigning themto their corresponding progenitors, thereby reconstructing the merger history. To track the subhalos and identify the particles asso ciated with them, we utilize two complementary pack ages for halo identification and merger-tree construc tion: Rockstar and Consistent Trees (CT). Rockstar isa phase-space halo finder that identifies (sub)halosby clustering particlesin six-dimensional phase space and assigning properties suchas mass and velocity (P. S. Behroozietal. 2012a; J. Samueletal. 2021). Rockstaris run onlyon the DM particles, and stars are assignedto each haloin postprocessing using similar phase-space criteria. We adopt Rockstarto construct the merger historyof each system, asit enables the associationof stars with their progenitor subhalos. In each snapshot, Rockstar defines the main",7 Selection criteria for GSE-analogs are discussedin Appendix,7 Selection criteria for GSE-analogs are discussedin Appendix,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_026,26,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto Appendix. B Diffuse Young untraceable Old untraceable MergerI Merger II Merger III Merger IV CT, such disagreements can leadto discrepanciesin the reconstructionof early merger events. In rare cases, a halo identifiedas the main haloby Rockstar may instead appearas merging into the CT main branch. Asare sult, the classificationof mergers occurring beforez ∼3 may therefore contain ambiguity. There are also casesin which our algorithm misidenti fies DM particlesas belonging toa merger when theydo not originate from the accreted subhalo, whichwe refertoas contamination. For each merger, we estimate this contaminationby identifying particles that are classifiedas belongingtoan accreted subhalo, but that wereal ready located within the host halo radius priorto the subhalo’s formal accretion. We then compute the con tamination fractionas the ratioof these particlesto the total numberof particles assignedto the merger. For more massive systems (Mpeak ≈1011M⊙), contamina tion from flybysorin situ particles within the main halo can account forupto 10% of particles identifiedasac creted. For lower-mass mergers (Mpeak ≈1010M⊙),",9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_027,27,"the ratioof these particlesto the total numberof particles assignedto the merger. For more massive systems (Mpeak ≈1011M⊙), contamina tion from flybysorin situ particles within the main halo can account forupto 10% of particles identifiedasac creted. For lower-mass mergers (Mpeak ≈1010M⊙), this contamination fractionis typically reducedto 1–5%. Additionally, a single physical subhalois occasionally fragmented into multiple subhalosin the merger tree. This can occur either dueto subhalo–subhalo interac tions that are not fully resolved intoa single object, or asa resultof tidal disruption near the main halo, where CT may lose trackof the original subhalo and instead assigna new identity. In such cases, we group all asso ciated particles intoa single “subhalo group” to ensure consistent treatmentin subsequent analyses. Morede tailed discussionof limitations willbe presentedin the Appendix. B. 1.0 0.8 0.6 Fraction 0.4 0.2 m12i m12f m12m m12w m12b m12c 0.0 Figure 3. Fractional contributionsof the individual com ponentsto the local DM population. The diffuse compo nentis generally the dominant contributor across the m12 sample. By contrast, the DM contributions from the top mergers—rankedby their associated stellar massin theso lar neighborhood—vary substantially, andin",9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_028,28,"Fractional contributionsof the individual com ponentsto the local DM population. The diffuse compo nentis generally the dominant contributor across the m12 sample. By contrast, the DM contributions from the top mergers—rankedby their associated stellar massin theso lar neighborhood—vary substantially, andin some cases canbe negligible relativeto the other components. The exact numerical valuesof all fractions are listedin Table. S2. contributionsof the individual componentsto the local DM populationis shownin Fig. 3.10. In this section, we describe how mergers are treated throughout the analysis. We beginby studying trace able mergersin Sec. 3.1. In Sec. 3.1.1, we introduce the statistical metric usedin this work, andin Secs. 3.1.2 and 3.1.3 we applyitto traceable mergersto quantify stellar–DM correlations. We then extend this analysisin Sec. 3.1.4, wherewe developan improved mapping from stellar kinematicsto the corresponding DM dis tributions for each progenitor. Finally, in Sec. 3.2, we discuss the treatmentof untraceable mergers and justify the assumptions adopted for their contributions.",9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,9 For consistency checkson the selection criterion please referto,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,198,152,False,False,280,432,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_029,29,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob jectsat various redshifts. Among these, the most signif icant contributorsto the local stellar population—those that deposita noticeable fractionof stars into the solar neighborhood—canbe tracked using their stellar coun terparts. Basedon this, we further classify the resolved mergers into three subcategories: (1) the top four merg ers that contribute the most stellar mass locally and canbe reliably tracked, whichwe call traceable mergers and are rankedby their contributionto the stellar con tentin the solar neighborhood, (2) young untraceable mergers that are recent and massive but lack clear stel lar signatures, and (3) old untraceable mergers that are too ancientor phase-mixedtobe associated with iden tifiable stellar debris. An illustrationof the fractional",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,173,133,False,False,0,133,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_030,30,"below the diagonal, confirming that the convolutionei ther improvesor preserves the original fit. Moreover, nearly all post-convolution EMD values fall below the 0.2 threshold, with onlya coupleof exceptions, indi cating that the convolved stellar distributions providea better approximationto the corresponding DM velocity distributionsin most cases. We plot the best fit valuesof µ for each merger asa functionof redshiftin Fig. 9. To enable meaningful comparisons across mergers, we normalize µ by the stel lar MAD asin previous sections. The majorityof the icant dependenceon redshiftor progenitor mass. This suggests thata systematic shift between the stellar and DM velocity distributionsis generally unnecessary. The best fit dispersionof the boosted Gaussian σ, also normalizedby stellar MAD, is shownin the bottom rowof Fig. 9. For the radial component, the normalized best fit σ is small (≲0.3) in general whichis expected since the differencein the velocity dispersionis smallas shownin Fig. 7. In contrast, σ of the angular compo nents exhibita clear trend with redshift, increasingat later times. Thusa gaussian convolutionin the radial componentis unnecessary, andwe will only apply the convolutionsto the angular componentsin the following analysis. We","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_031,31,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula tionis the diffuse component. This component includes both DM smoothly accreted onto the host halo andun resolved contributions from low-mass subhaloes below the resolution limitof the simulation (i.e. might not have enough star particlesto get correctly traced). For the Latte suite, we define the resolution threshold for resolved subhalosas 109M⊙in total mass. This thresh oldis scaled proportionally with the DM particle massin the lower- and higher-resolution versionsof each sim ulation, aswe discussin our resolutions tests (Sec. 6.2). The diffuse component constitutesa substantial frac tionof the local DM population—often exceeding the contribution from resolved mergers. The exact fraction varies significantly dependingon the specific accretion historyof the host halo and can range from approxi mately 40% toas highas 80% (see Tab.S2). As shownin Fig. 11, the diffuse component typically exhibitsa smoothly growing profile over time, with modest jumps that coincide with the accretion timesof major mergers. These featuresdo not solely imply significant misclassi ficationof merger-associated DM particlesas diffuseaswe",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_032,32,"80% (see Tab.S2). As shownin Fig. 11, the diffuse component typically exhibitsa smoothly growing profile over time, with modest jumps that coincide with the accretion timesof major mergers. These featuresdo not solely imply significant misclassi ficationof merger-associated DM particlesas diffuseaswe testedin Appendix. B.1, but rather reflect the rapid growthof the host’s virial radius during major accre tion events, which causes more particlesto fall within the host halo boundary andbe taggedas diffuse. Old Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.4 0.4 0.4 f(v ) [km/s] 1 f(v ) [km/s] 1 f(vr) [km/s] 1 0.3 0.3 0.3 0.2 0.2 0.2 0.1 0.1 0.1 vr/ vr 0.0 v / v 0.0 v / v 0.0 Young Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c 0.5 0.5 0.5 0.4 0.4 0.4 f(v ) [km/s] 1 f(v ) [km/s] 1 f(vr) [km/s] 1 0.3 0.3 0.3 0.2 0.2 0.2 0.1 0.1 0.1 vr/ vr 0.0 v / v 0.0 v / v 0.0 Figure 10. Velocity distributionsof untraceable mergers, separated into old and young populations. Old untraceable mergers",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
