astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_034,34,"stellar distributionas describedin Sec. 3.1.4, while the remaining material—including the diffuse compo nent and all untraceable mergers—is assumedto follow the generalized Gaussian form characterizedin Sec. 4. At this stage, the only remaining unknownin our reconstruction frameworkis the mass fractionof DM contributedby each mergerto the solar neighborhood. Observationally, itis extremely challengingto directly determine how much DM each progenitor depositslo cally. However, since DM and stars are accretedto gether—though with different spatial distributions and disruption timescales—itis plausible that the DM mass loss fraction relativeto the progenitoris correlated with the corresponding stellar mass loss fraction. Moreover, the total stellar massof each progenitor and its stellar debris are more accessible observationally, making the stellar mass loss fractiona potentially useful proxy for estimating the deposited DM. To investigate this, we compute the ratioof the DM loss fractionto the stellar loss fraction for each merger (Fig. 14). The ratio rises with increasing redshift before turning over aroundz ∼2, reflecting the different strip ping historiesof the two components. Because stars are Redshift Figure 11. Cumulative growthof the diffuse DM com ponent asa functionof accretion redshift, definedby the","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_035,35,"Gaussian profile, as done previously. The generalized Gaussian continuesto provide better fit than the standard Gaussian regardlessof resolution, confirming its robustness. The best-fit parameters for the low- and high-resolution simulations are also pre sentedin Table. 3, allof which also remain consistent with our fiducial results. Itis importantto note that for the standard- and low-resolution simulations, the quoted uncertainties include both the fitting errors and the sta tistical variance across the six m12 runs. In contrast, the high-resolution resultis derived from m12i alone, so the uncertainties reflect only the fitting error. These findings confirm that the phase-space structureof the diffuse DM component—and the associated gener alized Gaussian description—are robust against changesin numerical resolution. Dark Matter Stars 103f(vr)[km/s] 1 vr [km/s] Figure 18. Illustration ofa resolution-driven failure modein the EMD analysis fora low-mass mergerin the low-res olution m12w run. Shown are the stellar and DM velocity distributions associated with the fourth-ranked merger (by local stellar contribution). Owingto the limited numberof star and DM particlesin this event, the sampled velocity distributions become irregular and noise-dominated. Consequently, the orderingof mergersby local stellar contribution—particularly beyond",14 Itis importantto note that simulationsat different resolutions,14 Itis importantto note that simulationsat different resolutions,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_036,36,"stellar and DM velocity distributions associated with the fourth-ranked merger (by local stellar contribution). Owingto the limited numberof star and DM particlesin this event, the sampled velocity distributions become irregular and noise-dominated. Consequently, the orderingof mergersby local stellar contribution—particularly beyond the most dominant events—is not always preserved. For example, a merger labeled “4” or “5” ina low-resolution run need not cor respondto the same physical progenitoras the similarly labeled mergerin the fiducial-resolution simulation. Repeating our EMD analysis for these alternative resolution runs yields the results shownin Fig. 17. We find that the low-resolution simulations systematically exhibit slightly higher EMD values comparedto the standard Latte runs, though they generally follow the same redshift trend. Notably, some mergers display sig nificantly elevated EMDs—especiallyin the radial veloc ity component—indicativeof true resolution limitations. In particular, lower-mass events (often correspondingto the fourth-ranked mergerby local stellar contribution) canbe representedby too few stellar and dark-matter particles for their velocity distributionstobe robustly sampled. This undersampling can produce irregular, noise-dominated velocity histograms and consequently unreliable EMD estimates, as illustratedin Fig. 18. This observationis especially relevant when com paring our",14 Itis importantto note that simulationsat different resolutions,14 Itis importantto note that simulationsat different resolutions,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_037,37,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M Lisanti, Z. Mezghanni, T. Schpigel, N. Starkman for from NSF, via CAREER award AST-2337864. L.N. is supportedby the Sloan Fellowship and the NSF CA NSF, via CAREER award AST-2045928. AA acknowl edges support from Gordon and Betty Moore founda tion. Software: NumPy (C. R. Harrisetal. 2020), SciPy (P. Virtanenetal. 2020), Matplotlib (J. D. Hunter 2007), Pandas (W. McKinney 2010; T. pandas devel opment team 2020), Astropy ( Astropy Collaborationetal. 2013, 2018, 2022), GizmoAnalysis (A. Wetzel & S. Garrison-Kimmel 2020a), HaloAnalysis (A. Wetzel & S. Garrison-Kimmel 2020b), Rockstar (P. S. Behroozietal. 2012a), Consistent Trees (P. S. Behroozietal. 2012)","ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M","ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,151,116,False,False,0,116,False,2.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_000,0,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk galaxies, whilea key morphological feature for galaxy classifica tion, is similarly significant for driving galaxy evolution. Bars have been demonstratedto affect host-galaxy processeson all scales: from star formationon molecular-cloud scales (10 −100 pc; e.g. Kunoetal. 2000; Dobbsetal. 2006, 2014; Nilipouretal. 2024; Maedaetal. 2025) to galaxy-wide dynamics (∼1000 pc; e.g. Reganetal. 1999; O’Neill & Dubinski 2003; Emsellemetal. 2006; Ilesetal. 2024; Géronetal. 2024; Rutherfordetal. 2025); including disk–halo and disk–bulgeco-evolution (e.g. Athanassoula 2002; Valenzuela & Klypin 2003; Kormendy & Kennicutt 2004; Jogeeetal. 2005; Athanassoulaetal. 2013; Kruketal. 2018), angular momentum distribution (e.g. Weinberg 1985; Athanassoula 2005; Romeoetal. 2023), Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) (e.g. Shlosmanetal. 1989; Alonsoetal. 2014; Garlandetal. 2023), Inter-Stellar Medium (ISM) structure (Friedli & Benz 1993; Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettittetal. 2020; Katsiolietal. 2026), gas depletion and quenching (Mas tersetal. 2012; Spinosoetal. 2017; Fraser-McKelvieetal. 2020; Géronetal. 2021), for example. Accounting forupto ∼75% of nearby disk galaxies (e.g. Schinnereretal. 2002; Aguerrietal. 2009; Nair & Abraham 2010; Saha & Elmegreen 2018), an increasing num 2018;",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_001,1,"2026), gas depletion and quenching (Mas tersetal. 2012; Spinosoetal. 2017; Fraser-McKelvieetal. 2020; Géronetal. 2021), for example. Accounting forupto ∼75% of nearby disk galaxies (e.g. Schinnereretal. 2002; Aguerrietal. 2009; Nair & Abraham 2010; Saha & Elmegreen 2018), an increasing num 2018; Fosteretal. 2025) can reduce this variation but canbe simi larly inefficient and subjectiveif not applied carefully. More recently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn ing, via classification, regression, and segmentation (e.g. Abrahametal. 2018; Cavanagh & Bekki 2020; Fluke & Jacobs 2020; Ca vanaghetal. 2022; Huertas-Company & Lanusse 2023; Cavanaghetal. 2024), have become increasingly prevalent. However, since there areso many feasible methodsto identify the barin galaxies, itis necessaryto selecta ‘best’ method for each study. This presentsa fundamental problem: do anyof these methods consistently recover the same structure across all barred galaxies? The definition ofa bar, or barred region, is fundamentalto many studiesof host-galaxy properties, suchas gas flowor star formation along the bar (e.g. Downesetal. 1996; Shethetal. 2002; Momoseetal. 2010; Casasolaetal. 2011; Watanabeetal. 2019; Querejetaetal. 2021; Ilesetal. 2022), as wellas correlations between bar length and large-scale galactic properties, suchas longer barsin",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_002,2,"many studiesof host-galaxy properties, suchas gas flowor star formation along the bar (e.g. Downesetal. 1996; Shethetal. 2002; Momoseetal. 2010; Casasolaetal. 2011; Watanabeetal. 2019; Querejetaetal. 2021; Ilesetal. 2022), as wellas correlations between bar length and large-scale galactic properties, suchas longer barsin more massive galaxies (e.g. Kormendy 1979; Erwin 2005; Díaz-Garcíaetal. 2016), or shorter barsin late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985; Combes & Elmegreen 1993; Erwin 2005; Aguerrietal. 2009; Erwin 2019) and whether this maybe redshift dependent (e.g. Shethetal. 2008; Kimetal. 2021). If the definitionof the bar differs between methods, the physical trendswe observe maybe significantly effectedby this systematic bias. So, despite our abilityto define the barin many ways, defining the bar ina way thatis distinct, repeatable, and applicable across barred galaxies must still bea priority for the field. Itis onlyin this way thatwe can most effectively approacha unified understandingof bar structure, galaxy formation, and the roleof barsin driving galaxy evolution. ney & Tremaine 2008; Binney 2020), either directly from the stellar orbitsor via the instantaneous position and velocity measurements withan approximation ofa local Stäckel potential (e.g. Binney 2012; Sanders &",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_003,3,"& Kalnajs 1972; Carlberg & Sellwood 1985), can significantly alter individual actions; vertical action maybe conserved during radial migrationin general, but not necessarily ona star-by-star basis (e.g. Solwayetal. 2012; Vera-Ciro & D’Onghia 2016; Mikkolaetal. 2020); spiral perturbations can change azimuthal action while leaving radialac tion nearly unchanged (e.g. Sellwood & Binney 2002; Roškaretal. 2012); and rapid potential evolution, suchas during bar formation and buckling (e.g. Toomre 1981; Sellwood 1981; Thielheim & Wolff 1982; Rahaetal. 1991), can violate adiabatic invariance entirely. Assumptions and approximations have soughtto mitigate the effectsof this varianceto probe galactic history (McMillan 2011; Binney 2012; Sanders & Binney 2015; Antojaetal. 2018; Ting & Rix 2019; Malhanetal. 2022; Carlbergetal. 2024), while recent studies have startedto explore how resonances and the action distributionof stars are relatedto bars, spirals, and boxy/peanut bulges (e.g. Debattistaetal. 2020; Kawataetal. 2021; Tricketal. 2021; Trick 2022; Drim meletal. 2023; Zozuliaetal. 2024; Arunimaetal. 2025). In this work, we are only concerned with exploiting the existenceof these changesto the action, rather than reducingor categorising theiref fects. The premise hereis: if actions are systematically not conserved for bar-located stars",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_004,4,"Drim meletal. 2023; Zozuliaetal. 2024; Arunimaetal. 2025). In this work, we are only concerned with exploiting the existenceof these changesto the action, rather than reducingor categorising theiref fects. The premise hereis: if actions are systematically not conserved for bar-located stars but remain broadly conserved elsewherein the disc, then the non-conservation itself becomesa diagnostic. This will allowusto directly infer bar membership and identify bar properties througha simple assessmentof the conservationof action for stars throughout the galactic potential. In this work, we demonstrate this methodin the contextof numer ical 𝑁−body simulations, making the best useof simulated datato track time-evolution. Ina companion paper (see also Ilesetal. 2026) we demonstrate the applicationof this method for the Milky Way bar, using observationsof starsin large surveys. A descriptionof the method and test simulationis includedin Section 2. The definition ofa standard bar region (e.g. radial bar extent and pitch-angle) is includedin Section 3, while Section 4 outlines the ‘star-by-star’ use case, identifying potential bar membership for each stellar particlein the simulation. Finally, our conclusions and recommendations for future applicationsof this method are includedin Section 5.",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,232,178,False,False,700,878,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_005,5,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using 𝑁−body SPH (smoothed particle hydrodynamics) in the formofan isolated disk galaxy simulation (IsoB) from Ilesetal. (2022). To test bar-finding ona star-by-star basis, simulations with high tem poral and spatial resolution were necessarytobe ableto trace bar evolution and stellar orbits. The IsoB simulation hasa stellar particle mass resolutionof 1044 M⊙for simulation-formed stars, a stellar softening lengthof 50 pc anda snapshot for every 10 Myrof galaxy evolution, whichis approximately equivalentto the star formation timescale (𝑡SF = 10 Myr). This disk was evolved with gasoline2 (Wadsleyetal. 2017), comprisinga dark matter component, a small stellar bulge, an initial stellar disk anda gas disk componentof parti cles respectively, following the standard sub-grid prescriptions (Katzetal. 1996; Stinsonetal. 2006); with super-bubble feedback (Kelleretal. 2014), UV and photoelectric heating, as wellas metal coolingin the form ofa tabulated cooling function (Shenetal. 2010), re covering the two-phase ISM (Wolfireetal. 2003) froman initially isothermal (104 K) gas profile. Star formation was prescribed bya standard temperature (300 K) and density (100",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_006,6,"photoelectric heating, as wellas metal coolingin the form ofa tabulated cooling function (Shenetal. 2010), re covering the two-phase ISM (Wolfireetal. 2003) froman initially isothermal (104 K) gas profile. Star formation was prescribed bya standard temperature (300 K) and density (100 atoms/cc) threshold, as well asa convergent flow requirement withan efficiencyof ∼10% anda Chabrier (2003) initial mass function (IMF). The initial condi tion was originally tailoredto observationsof nearby, resolved galaxy ngc4303 via the rotation curve, gas and stellar mass fractions (see Ilesetal. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved fora relatively short periodof 1 Gyr, however, thisis sufficientto covera period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, anda shortpe riod post-bar formationto capture action propertiesin eachof these regimes. Later snapshotsof the IsoB disk canbe considereda classic example ofa barred galaxy, however, automated bar identification methodsdo not consistently produce realistic bar parameters for this diskin every snapshot. Thus, we consider this simulationa good elementary demonstrationof the proposed method, as there are suf ficient snapshots with automated bar classification for comparison but automation can struggle with this galaxy. Additionally, we have recently completed",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_007,7,"lines are usedto visually indicate where the total action changesby half the original amount. We will use this percentage changein total action (Δ𝐽tot%) parameter throughout the following analysis. As the levelof detailin the colour distributionof each panel becomes increasingly blurred (similar colour spreads across the image) over larger time periods (increasing downward), we select the smallest period (Δ𝑡= 10 ) as the standard for all future analysis. Thisis becausewe intendto exploit the conservationof actionto constrain disk properties with the highest possible precision. However, by eyeit would appear that the Δ𝑡≤100 Myr window still retainsat least the large-scale featuresin this representation. Usinga simple model ofa flat rotation curveat the maximum rotational velocity (𝑉circ ∼200 kms−1) and the mid disk radius (𝑅∼5 kpc), we can findan approximate rotational period for the disk (𝑇disk ∼150 Myr), whichis likely the limit (Δ𝑡≤𝑇disk) at which structural variation using this method couldbe acquired. Finally, we note that defining the axisymmetric formof the galactic potentialis fundamentalto the calculationof the actions. Thuswe also",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,215,165,False,True,560,725,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_008,8,"for this diskin every snapshot. Thus, we consider this simulationa good elementary demonstrationof the proposed method, as there are suf ficient snapshots with automated bar classification for comparison but automation can struggle with this galaxy. Additionally, we have recently completed visual classification for each snapshotof the IsoB simulation fora study into visual bar classification (Ilesetal. 2025) which, despite some limitations, can alsobe used asa comparison for the bar parametersof this diskas measured ‘by-eye’. Figure 1. The percentage changein total action 𝐽tot for time periods Δ𝑡= 10, 50, 100, 300 Myr increasing downwardin each panel. The colour isa measureof the numberof star particles existingat both the start and endof the period measured (dark = less, light = more) and the scaleis logarithmic. Thisis the isolated simulation (IsoB). Fine details obviousin the upper panel increasingly blurin lower panels. inherently bea measurementof change over some time interval. To determine the most appropriate period (Δ𝑡), we measured the changein total action (𝐽tot) overa rangeof time periods (Δ𝑡= 10, 50, 100, 300 Myr) which are fractions (0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3) of our total simulation time",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,True,4.5
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_009,9,"structure, galaxy formation, and the roleof barsin driving galaxy evolution. ney & Tremaine 2008; Binney 2020), either directly from the stellar orbitsor via the instantaneous position and velocity measurements withan approximation ofa local Stäckel potential (e.g. Binney 2012; Sanders & Binney 2015), and algorithms that compute actions are readily available, relatively widespread and mostly computationally inexpensive, practical even for million-particle 𝑁−body simulationsor extremely large observational datasets (e.g. Debattistaetal. 2020; Mikkolaetal. 2020; Zozuliaetal. 2024). The most useful invariant propertyof the action, however, relies primarilyon the assumption that the potentialis axisymmetric and invariant. Actions ceasetobe conservedin non-axisymmetric envi ronments, suchas bars and spiral arms (e.g. Binney & Tremaine 2008; Solwayetal. 2012; Vera-Ciro & D’Onghia 2016); resonances, suchasco-rotation (CR; e.g. Sellwood & Binney 2002; Roškaretal. 2012; Daniel & Wyse 2015) and the inner- and outer-Lindblad resonances (ILR/OLR; e.g. Barbanis & Woltjer 1967; Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs 1972; Carlberg & Sellwood 1985), can significantly alter individual actions; vertical action maybe conserved during radial migrationin general, but not necessarily ona star-by-star basis (e.g. Solwayetal. 2012; Vera-Ciro & D’Onghia 2016; Mikkolaetal. 2020); spiral perturbations can change azimuthal",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_010,10,"over some time interval. To determine the most appropriate period (Δ𝑡), we measured the changein total action (𝐽tot) overa rangeof time periods (Δ𝑡= 10, 50, 100, 300 Myr) which are fractions (0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3) of our total simulation time respectively. The resultsof this test are presentedin Figure 1. This figure isa histogramof the changein total action (Δ𝐽tot) over each Δ𝑡value (panels, increasing downward) presented asa heat mapat each resolved snapshotof the simulation (every 10 Myr), with time also increasing downward along the 𝑦−axis and starting from 𝑡= 100 Myr for divisibility, as wellasto avoid any settling effectsin the start-upof the simulation. On the 𝑥-axis, the percentage changein total action ((𝐽tot(𝑡final) −𝐽tot(𝑡initial))/𝐽tot(𝑡initial)%) is used asa value independent metric for categorising the extentof action change. over the period specified, whilea valueof 100 indicatesa 100% changein the valueof the action. Dashed white lines are usedto visually indicate where the total action changesby half the original amount. We will use this percentage changein total action (Δ𝐽tot%) parameter throughout the following analysis. As the levelof detailin the colour distributionof each panel becomes increasingly blurred",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_011,11,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation ElizabethJ. Iles,1,2★FinnA. Pal,2 Joss Bland-Hawthorn,1 and Ken Freeman3 1Sydney Institute for Astronomy (SIfA), Sydney University, Camperdown 2050, Australia 2Schoolof Physics, Universityof New South Wales, Kensington 2052, Australia 3Research Schoolof Astronomy & Astrophysics, Mount Stromlo Observatory, Cotter Road, Weston Creek, ACT 2611, Australia ABSTRACTA bar-like central featureis commonly observedin both nearby and distant spiral-type galaxies, including the Milky Way. a rigorous and consistent method for identifying these bars, we investigatea classification scheme basedon dynamical actions. In the Gaia era, actions canbe estimated for individual starsin both observations and simulations, making thisa natural and unifying diagnostic, assuming the intrinsic errors and selection functions are understood. Our approachis straightforward: stars that participatein the bar are subject toa strongly non-axisymmetric potential and, therefore, do not completely conserve their actions. We use this propertyto definea star-by-star criterion, formulatedasan inequality and evaluated within measurement uncertainties, to identify bar members basedon the degreeto which their total action failstobe conserved. From testson simulated galaxies, we find that the bar regionis",Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_012,12,"conserve their actions. We use this propertyto definea star-by-star criterion, formulatedasan inequality and evaluated within measurement uncertainties, to identify bar members basedon the degreeto which their total action failstobe conserved. From testson simulated galaxies, we find that the bar regionis indeed characterised bya lower fractionof stars with conserved actions and that starson bar orbits are representedby larger percentage changesin their actions. We are ableto classify the spatial extentof barred region via the standard parametersof bar length and orientation, while also individually separating bar-located from bar-member starson bar orbits. As proofof concept, our automated method basedon dynamical actions robustly identifies bar parameters that closely match the eye’s performance (average bar length variation ∼9%) in barred snapshotsof the test galaxy. Key words: galaxies: structure – galaxies: fundamental parameters – galaxies: kinematics and dynamics berof galaxiesat high redshift (e.g. Guoetal. 2023; Costantinetal. 2023; Liangetal. 2024; Le Conteetal. 2024, 2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakadaetal. 1991; Paczyn skietal. 1994; Zhaoetal. 1994; Bland-Hawthorn & Gerhard 2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still",Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_013,13,"Liangetal. 2024; Le Conteetal. 2024, 2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakadaetal. 1991; Paczyn skietal. 1994; Zhaoetal. 1994; Bland-Hawthorn & Gerhard 2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still lacka distinct, universally-applicable method usedto define the spatial extentof the “bar” in these galaxies (see, for recent example: Dehnenetal. 2023; Luceyetal. 2023; Petersenetal. 2024; Ilesetal. 2025), let aloneto identify whetheranin dividual star isa memberof the baror just passing through. For example, Fourier analysis (e.g. Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985; Ohtaetal. 1990; Aguerrietal. 1998; Garcia-Gómezetal. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosstetal. 2025) has long been usedto identify bars, particularlyin simulations, while observational studies have traditionally reliedon isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abrahametal. 1999; Laineetal. 2002; Erwin 2005; Gadotti & de Souza 2006; Mari nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestreetal. 2007; Consolandi 2016; Jiangetal. 2018) or photometric decomposition (e.g. Reeseetal. 2007; Gadotti 2008; Durbalaetal. 2008, 2009; Weinzirletal. 2009; Kruketal. 2018; Fraser-McKelvieetal. 2025). Visual clas sification (e.g. Nair & Abraham 2010; Hoyleetal. 2011; Mastersetal. 2011, 2012; Melvinetal. 2014; Kruketal. 2018; Mastersetal. 2021; Géronetal. 2021) remains common,",Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_014,14,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies, particularly aimedat identifying barsin simulations with high res olution capturing complexor non-standard systems, suchas double bars, nuclear disks, or bars distortedby interactions, with the added capacityto track time evolutionon galactic scales. Further, this pro cessis designedbe ableto determine, not only the spatial extentof the bar but also, the membershipof individual starsto the bar, dis tinguishing starson bar-supporting orbits, or orbits affectedby the bar, from those merely passing through. This capability will enable the assessmentof important bar-driven processes, including star for mation, radial migration and chemical mixing, ona more precise, star-by-star basis, at leastin simulations. The basisof this methodisto exploit the intrinsic propertiesof the angle–action variables (𝜃𝑖, 𝐽𝑖) in different regionsof the galacticpo tential. Action-space, describedby these angle-action variables, has long been usedto probe many propertiesof the Milky Way through Galactic dynamics (e.g. Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs 1972; Kalnajs 1977; Arnold 1978; Martinetetal. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein berg 2001; Sellwood 2012). Thisis primarily dueto the convenienceof the action integrals, asa","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_015,15,"been usedto probe many propertiesof the Milky Way through Galactic dynamics (e.g. Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs 1972; Kalnajs 1977; Arnold 1978; Martinetetal. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein berg 2001; Sellwood 2012). Thisis primarily dueto the convenienceof the action integrals, asa setof adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit ina steady, or slowly varying, potential (see Binney & Tremaine 2008, fora more specific detail). Actions have been instrumentalin modelling phase-mixed populations (e.g. Tremaine 1999; Antojaetal. 2018; Ting & Rix 2019; Monarietal. 2019; Drimmeletal. 2023), identifying stellar streams (e.g. Helmi & White 1999; Eyre & Binney 2011; Sanders & Binney 2013), and bet ter constraining the dark matter distribution within the Galaxy (e.g. Machado & Manos 2016; Malhanetal. 2022; Carlbergetal. 2024). Most importantly, the more abstract action-angleco-ordinates canbe easily relatedto ordinary phase-space variables (𝑥, 𝑣) (e.g. Bin","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,176,135,False,False,140,275,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_016,16,"three stars are indicativeof each: a mostly circular disk orbit (𝑅≳5 kpc), a bar-orbit (3 ≳𝑅≳0.5 kpc), anda centralor bit (𝑅≲0.5 kpc). These star particles were randomly selected based purelyon their changein total action percentage (Δ𝐽tot%) asa meansto demonstrate the additional powerof this method for broadly sep arating orbits that commonly inhabit different spatial/morphological regionsof the galactic disk, without havingto analyse the full orbitsof each star. As such, with this methoditis possibleto define the bar regionin projection via standard bar parameters, while similarly exploiting the capabilityto separate the bar-located and bar-member stellar populations basedon the star-by-star valuesof the total changein action. Thisis practical for simulation studies where all stellar particles are resolved and change canbe measured directly from snapshot-to-snapshot time evolution. However, aswe demonstratein (Ilesetal. 2026) this concept can alsobe exploitedin observations where actions are similarly calculated ona star-by-star basis, suchas within the Milky Way.",4 STAR-BY-STAR CLASSIFICATION The benefitof using the conservationof actionto classify the baris,4 STAR-BY-STAR CLASSIFICATION The benefitof using the conservationof actionto classify the baris,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,189,145,False,False,140,285,False,3.0
