astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_000,0,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers Recent research indicatesa noticeable declinein the rotation curve (RC) of the Milky Way (MW), suggesting the need fora new low-mass modelto describe its dynamical features. This study constructsa new Gaia-Sausage-Enceladus (GSE) merger modelto characterize the RC featuresof our galaxy. We use the GIZMO codeto simulate mergers with various orbital parametersto investigate how the disk warp evolves under different conditions. This simulation demonstrates the evolutionary mechanismof disk warp, which arises dueto the asymmetric gravitational potentialof the dark matter (DM) halo generated universallyby galaxy mergers. The results indicate that the tilt angleof the DM halo partly reflects the gravitational strengthat theZ = 0 plane, while the gravitational strengthon the disk plane reflects the amplitudeof disk warp. We identifya dual-regime interaction mechanism drivenby the asymmetric halo potential. On short timescales, we finda distinct anti-correlation between the halo’s tilt angle and the disk’s warp amplitude, indicatinga ‘seesaw’ mechanismof angular momentum exchange. On secular timescales, however, dynamical",ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers,ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_001,1,"disk warp. We identifya dual-regime interaction mechanism drivenby the asymmetric halo potential. On short timescales, we finda distinct anti-correlation between the halo’s tilt angle and the disk’s warp amplitude, indicatinga ‘seesaw’ mechanismof angular momentum exchange. On secular timescales, however, dynamical friction drivesa global alignment, causing both the halo tilt and the warp amplitudeto decay simultaneously. Furthermore, we demonstrate that high-inclination mergers can sustain long-lived prograde precession, where the persistent yet decaying gravitational torque maintains the prograde bending mode against differential wind-up. Keywords: Galaxy structure (622); Milky Way disk (1050); Milky Way dark matter halo (1049); Galaxy mergers (608); Hydrodynamical simulations (767)",ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers,ABSTRACT Previous studies have shown that disk warps can result from galaxy mergers,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,132,101,False,False,140,241,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_002,2,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.MODEL AND SIMULATION In our previous work (Dengetal. 2024), we developeda realistic merger model for the MW. This model successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp, capturing almost all its observed features. However, recent studies (Jiaoetal. 2023; Ouetal. 2023) suggest that the MW rotation curve declines more rapidlyat larger radii. Therefore, an improved modelis neededto accurately represent the mass distributionof the galaxy. The numerical initial conditions (ICs) were generated using the Disk Initial Conditions Environment (DICE) code (Perretetal. 2014; Perret 2016). Density profiles for DM, stars, and gas were input into DICE as distribution functions. These functions were then usedto generate Lagrangian particles via the Metropolis-Hastings Monte Carlo Markov Chain algorithm (Metropolisetal. 1953). The mass resolutionis 1 × 105 M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolutionis sufficientto track the warp structures, as the presenceof warp has also been observedin some lower resolution cosmological simulation suchas TNG100 (Semczuketal. 2020). The modeling approachin this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implementeda star formation and feedback",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_003,3,"resolutionis sufficientto track the warp structures, as the presenceof warp has also been observedin some lower resolution cosmological simulation suchas TNG100 (Semczuketal. 2020). The modeling approachin this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implementeda star formation and feedback module following Wangetal. (2012), enabling gas particlesto convert into stellar particles and vice versa through feedback mechanisms. GIZMO isa variantof the smooth particle hydrodynamics code Gadget-3, employing Adaptive Gravity Softening (see detailsin Hopkinsetal. 2018). 2.1. GSE Our starting pointis basedon GSE mass measuredby Laneetal. (2023). Using high-purity samplesof APOGEE DR16 red giants selectedby kinematics and chemistry, they estimated the massof the GSE remnantas 1.45+0.92 −0.51 × 108 M⊙, witha total mass ∼6 × 1010 M⊙. The GSE model consists ofa stellar disk, a gas disk, anda DM halo. Considering mass loss during the merger process, the total massof the GSE progenitor usedin the simulationis setto 7.2 × 1010 M⊙, whichis 20% higher than the reference value (Leeetal. 2018). The stellar and gas disk models Table 1. Initial Conditions atz ∼2 Progenitor Parameters Milky Way GSE Units Dark Matter mass 11.55",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_004,4,"progenitor usedin the simulationis setto 7.2 × 1010 M⊙, whichis 20% higher than the reference value (Leeetal. 2018). The stellar and gas disk models Table 1. Initial Conditions atz ∼2 Progenitor Parameters Milky Way GSE Units Dark Matter mass 11.55 6.93 1 × 1010 M⊙ α 3.5 3.25 N/A Dark Matter scale radius 12.867 8.02 kpc Stellar disk mass 0.2 1 × 109 M⊙ Stellar scale length 0.6 kpc Stellar scale height 0.8 0.3 kpc Gas disk mass 2.5 1 × 109 M⊙ Gas scale length 2.4 kpc Gas scale height 1.2 kpc Bulge mass 19.5 N/A 1 × 109 M⊙ Bulge scale 0.9 N/A kpc Orbital Parameters Eccentricity (e) 0.9 Initial distance 100.5 kpc Inclination angle 15◦, 30◦, 45◦, 55◦, 60◦, 75◦ degree Relative velocity 73.02 km/s followan exponential + sech-z profile. The stellar massis setto the median reference valueof 2 × 108 M⊙. The stellar disk scale lengthR⋆(approximately 0.6 kpc), is determined using the size-mass relation (SMR) from Mowlaetal. (2019). The disk scale heightis 0.5 × R⋆, basedon simulations showing thatz ∼2 disks become thick dueto turbulent gas",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_005,5,"To simplify the calculations, we neglect the time derivative term ∂hw ∂t and minimize the lengthof theR bin and assume that ω remains constant with respect toR. Although the warpis evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effecton the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model canbe expressedin the same formas Equation 7 in Poggioetal. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ) = (Vϕ R −ω)hw(R)cos(ϕ −ϕw). (10) Using this kinematic model, once the geometric shapeis determinedby fitting Eq.7, we can substitute the parametershw and ϕ to measure the precession rate ω basedon the simulation’s kinematic data. For this analysis, we select young stars (age < 1.5 Gyr) within the radial rangeR = 7 −17 kpc. The regionis then divided into 20 radial binsto track and plot the temporal evolutionof ω. Figure 5 shows time evolutionof stellar disk precession across different inclination groups. Long-term prograde precessionis more frequently observedin the high-inclination group, although transient high prograde precessionis also occasionally observedin the low-inclination group, it appears asa temporary feature andis generally dominatedby retrograde precession for the majorityof the simulation time. The disk warp",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_006,6,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model In our previous study, we successfully reproduced the disk warp usinga gas-rich GSE merger model, in which the progenitor galaxy was modeled following Naiduetal. (2021). In that model, the DM halo was represented usinga Hernquist profile (Hernquist 1990) anda standard flattened distribution. In the current work, we adoptan Einasto profileto reproduce the declining rotation curve, which lead toa different gravitational potentialon the disk plane. Figure 11 shows comparisonof two different model’s evolutionof disk warp amplitude, which are both ona 55◦ inclination, clearly indicating that the flattened DM halo model producesa stronger warp. We mark the moments when each model reaches its maximum amplitude and the MW-like warp amplitude with dotsin the top panelof Figure 11. The bottom panels compare them = 1 mode acceleration amplitudeson the disk plane. The left panel shows the MW-like warp, withan amplitudeof approximately ∼1 kpc atR = 16 kpc), where solid lines represent the DM acceleration withinR ≤20 kpc and the DM mass",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_007,7,"compare them = 1 mode acceleration amplitudeson the disk plane. The left panel shows the MW-like warp, withan amplitudeof approximately ∼1 kpc atR = 16 kpc), where solid lines represent the DM acceleration withinR ≤20 kpc and the DM mass for decline modelis about 1.3 × 1011 M⊙, for the flatten modelis 1.9 × 1011 M⊙, and dashed lines show the acceleration from 20 kpc ≤R ≤50 kpc, the DM massin this rangeis about 3.3 × 1010 M⊙and 2.24 × 1011 M⊙for decline and flatten model respectively. We observe that similar warp amplitudes can result from similar acceleration magnitudes, evenin different mass distribution models. In the declining DM model, mostof the massis concentratedin the inner region (R ≤2.5 kpc), resultingin gravitational acceleration amplitudes that arean orderof magnitude higher than thoseof the flattened modelin the inner region. Specifically, the outer halo accelerationis oneto two ordersof magnitude weaker than that from the central region. The right panel shows the maximum warp amplitudes for each model: approximately ∼5.4 kpc for the flattened model and ∼2.7 kpc for the declining model. While the outer",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_008,8,"accelerationis oneto two ordersof magnitude weaker than that from the central region. The right panel shows the maximum warp amplitudes for each model: approximately ∼5.4 kpc for the flattened model and ∼2.7 kpc for the declining model. While the outer halo acceleration remains nearly unchanged, the central DM halo showsa significant increasein acceleration. The central acceleration amplitudein the flattened modelis roughly twice thatof the declining model. As shownin Figure 11, although the models adopt different DM halo profiles, the resulting disk warps are drivenby similar gravitational strengths originating from the inner halo. An increasein accelerationon the disk plane correspondstoan increasein disk warp amplitude. Although the decline model may notbe sufficienton its ownto fully explain the evolutionof the MW warp during its later stages, its contribution should notbe overlooked. The model may still account for partof the present-day warp amplitude. Another scenariois proposedby Koopetal. (2024), who suggest that repeated perturbations from satellite passages have significantly disturbed the MW disk, rendering its rotation curve unreliable for mass estimation. This might imply that the halo profileis still flattenedin reality.",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,229,176,False,False,280,456,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_009,9,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 4.2.Kinematic warp model In the previous subsection, we examined the evolutionof the Galactic warp, emphasizing that its rapid changesin amplitude reveal the inherently dynamical natureof warps. Understanding this dynamismis essential for tracing the formation historyof galaxies and determining the mass distributionof their halos. The changing geometryof the warp canbe characterizedby the variationin the directionof its linesof nodes (LONs), which precess ata rate ω. This relationshipis givenby ϕw(t) = ϕw,0 + ωt where ϕw,0 denotes the current positionof the LONs. A time-dependent warp model basedon this frameworkis presentedby Poggioetal. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ, t = 0) = (Vϕ R −ω(R))hw(R)cos(ϕ −ϕw) + ∂hw ∂t sin(ϕ −ϕw). (9) Herehw(R) = a(R−Rw)b, as describedin Eq. 1, VZ represents the mean vertical velocity and Vϕ represents the mean azimuthal velocity. To simplify the calculations, we neglect the time derivative term ∂hw ∂t and minimize the lengthof theR bin and assume that ω remains constant with respect toR. Although the warpis evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effecton",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,4.5
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_010,10,"valueof 2 × 108 M⊙. The stellar disk scale lengthR⋆(approximately 0.6 kpc), is determined using the size-mass relation (SMR) from Mowlaetal. (2019). The disk scale heightis 0.5 × R⋆, basedon simulations showing thatz ∼2 disks become thick dueto turbulent gas and remain thick (Parketal. 2021). Given thatit isa gas-rich merger atz ∼2, we assumea high gas fractionof 0.92 for the GSE. Following assumptions from galaxy merger and metallicity gradient simulationsby Coxetal. (2008) and Rochaetal. (2008), the gas disk scale lengthis setto 4 × R⋆, witha scale height equalto halfof this length.",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,119,91,False,False,420,511,True,4.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_011,11,"the frequencyof vertical oscillation contributedby the halo potential. This defines, form = 1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−νh < Ωp < Ω+νh, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν > Ω, so the ‘fast’ waveis prograde, witha frequency ω depending strongly onR for most radii, so differential rotation windsitup rapidly andit decays. The ‘slow’ wave, on the other hand, is retrograde and circulates with frequency only weakly depending onR for most radii. This waveis thus expectedto windup slowly andbe long-lived. In other words, prograde waves generally disappear quickly, except for persistent disturbances. In addition, accordingto Eq. 10, a positive ω (prograde precession) leads toa reductionin the vertical velocityof the disk. Conversely, retrograde precession increases the vertical velocity. Therefore, when the diskis ina high prograde but transient precession phase, the warp amplitude often reachesa minimum ina short timeof its evolution, anda low prograde but long-lasting precession, the warp amplitudeisin the processof continuous attenuation For example, these behaviors canbe seen during the periodsof 3 −3.5 Gyr and 5 −5.5 Gyrin the 15◦inclination, and during the periodof 3 −11 Gyrof the 75◦inclinationin Figure 5",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,True,4.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_012,12,"groups. Long-term prograde precessionis more frequently observedin the high-inclination group, although transient high prograde precessionis also occasionally observedin the low-inclination group, it appears asa temporary feature andis generally dominatedby retrograde precession for the majorityof the simulation time. The disk warp canbe describedas anm = 1 bending wave propagating ina kinematically cool disk. Sucha wave canbe viewedas the superpositionof ‘fast’ (+) and ‘slow’ (−) waves circulating with frequencies ω = mΩ(R) ± ν(R), where Ω(R) is the angular rotation curve, ν(R) is the vertical frequency, andm-fold rotational symmetryis assumed. The inclusionof the disk’s selfgravity raises ν(R), making bending waves more stable, i.e. stiffer (contraryto density waves) – see Binney & Tremaine (2008). Fora given rotation curve ω(R), bending waves can only propagatein regions that satisfy the condition [ΩP −Ω(R)]2 ≥ν2 h. (11) where Ωp = ω/mis the pattern speed and νhis the frequencyof vertical oscillation contributedby the halo potential. This defines, form = 1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−νh < Ωp < Ω+νh, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν > Ω, so the ‘fast’ waveis prograde, witha frequency ω depending strongly",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,True,3.5
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_013,13,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.2.Milky Way The MW massis determined basedon the hypothesis that the GSE merger wasa major merger witha mass ratioof 2.5: 1 (Belokurovetal. 2018; Naiduetal. 2021). Therefore, the virial massof the MW is settobe 1.8 × 1011 M⊙. Additionally, we requirean appropriate representationof the MW during the merger epoch. Ages derived from various methods suggest that nearly allof the current high-α/thick disk and the bulge formed atz > 1. Therefore, we model the MW atz ∼2 asa combinationof the present-day thick disk, gas disk, and bulge. Xiangetal. (2025) studied the age-dependent structure and star formation rateof the MW disk using high-α stars with substantial orbital angular momentum and precise age determinations. Referringto Fig. 4 in their work, we set the stellar disk massat approximately 1.5 × 1010 M⊙atz ∼2. Using the size-mass relation (SMR), the disk scale length (R⋆) is settobe 2 kpc. The disk thicknessis setto 0.4 × R⋆, basedon Fig. 3 in Xiangetal. (2025). The gas fractionof the MW progenitoris setat 0.667. The scale",2.2.Milky Way,2.2.Milky Way,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_014,14,"1010 M⊙atz ∼2. Using the size-mass relation (SMR), the disk scale length (R⋆) is settobe 2 kpc. The disk thicknessis setto 0.4 × R⋆, basedon Fig. 3 in Xiangetal. (2025). The gas fractionof the MW progenitoris setat 0.667. The scale lengthof the gas diskis 3 × R⋆, witha thicknessof half scale length. Both disks followan exponential + sech-z profile. For the MW bulge atz ∼2, we adopt the Bulge-E model from Jiaoetal. (2023), witha massof 1.95 × 1010 M⊙anda scale length (Rb) of 0.9 kpc. The bulgeis modeled usingan Einasto profile (Einasto 1965), analogousto theS´ersic profile (S´ersic 1968) but applicableto three-dimensional mass density distributions (Coe 2010). This choiceis reasonable given that theS´ersic profileis widely usedto fit galaxy surface densities. Combining the above components, the total massis setat 6.72 × 1010 M⊙. This value aligns with the baryonic massof approximately 6 × 1010 M⊙reportedby Jiaoetal. (2023) and Ouetal. (2023).",2.2.Milky Way,2.2.Milky Way,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,193,148,True,False,140,288,False,3.0
