astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_017,17,"lines indicate the amplitudeof MW in observation. High-inclination models show larger initial amplitudes, and the warp displaysa regeneration phenomenon: the amplitude often decays toa near-flat state before rising again toa new peak. two measurements will gradually become consistent. Regarding the measurementof precession, we found that thereis not much difference between the two sampling methods. Therefore, the specific choiceof sampling does not affect our final conclusions. Overall, we maintain that choosing young stars ensures the robustnessof our results while being more realistic with observations. Since the Galactic stellar and gas disks exhibit similar warp amplitudes, the stellar and gas disks within the same inclination group also show comparable evolutionary trendsin Figure 4. This suggests they are dominatedby the same evolutionary mechanismor influencedby the same gravitational potential. In high-inclination groups, the warp amplitudeis larger during the early stages, possibly dueto the higher vertical angular momentumof the merger orbit. Most notably, a regeneration mechanism appearsin the warp evolution. At times, the disk warp amplitude declines toa near-flat state, onlyto regenerate and rise again toa new peak. Since the simulation involves onlya single initial",4.1.Evolutionof disk warp,4.1.Evolutionof disk warp,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_018,18,"disturbancein our modelis the gravitational influenceof the DM halo. In this merger model, the dynamic coupling between the DM halo and the diskin the later evolutionary stages, resultingin reduced gravitational disturbance. This trendis evidentin Figure 4 and the right panelof Figure 9. During the evolution, the disk warp undergoes repeated cyclesof disappearance and regeneration. However, the peak amplitudeof each regeneration event gradually decreases over time, which agrees with the observed general decreasein warp amplitude with decreasing redshift (Vladimiretal. 2025). Since gravitational disturbance scales with mass, this low-mass model strugglesto produce high warp amplitudesin the later stages. As notedin Section 4.2, observed prograde precession tendsto reduce vertical velocity and thereby suppress warp amplitude. This suggests that the Galactic warp may have hada larger amplitudein the past. Therefore, an additional sourceof gravitational disturbance maybe requiredto fully reproduce the Galactic warp. Previous studies suggest that the Sagittarius (Sgr) dwarf galaxy and the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) could also induce disk warp (Steleaetal. 2024). Some studies also report retrograde precessionin observations (Huangetal. 2024), or more complex precession patterns that vary between low prograde and",5.1.Comparing with the Galactic warp,5.1.Comparing with the Galactic warp,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
