astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_021,21,"and 9.5 −10.5 Gyr. This inconsistency may stem from limited spatial resolution. The gravitational field was calculated ona 100 × 100 grid, whereas displacement and velocity were computed using all thin disk particles within the same spatial range. The mismatchin spatial resolution and the dispersionof disk particles likely contributeto phase inconsistenciesin the later stages, where the warp amplitude also decreases, as shownin Figure 4. The velocity phaseof Figure 8 showsa phase offsetof approximately 90◦relativeto the acceleration. For example, regions with phase ∼−50◦(green) in the velocity map correspondto phase ∼50◦(blue) in the acceleration map. Additionally, the temporal evolutionof the two phases shows similar trends. For instance, around 6.9 Gyr, both acceleration and velocity phases are decreasing, from green (∼−50◦) to yellow (∼−100◦) for the velocity phase and acceleration phase changes from blue (∼50◦) to light blue (∼0◦), while near ∼8 Gyr, both beginto increase simultaneously, from greento light blue for the velocity and from blueto deep blue (∼75◦). Vz 1 (deg) Z 1 (deg) R (kpc) R (kpc) Simulation Time (Gyr) Simulation Time (Gyr) Diskaz 1 (deg) R (kpc) Simulation Time",4.3.Perturbation from the DM halo,4.3.Perturbation from the DM halo,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440,False,2.0
